# Hypothesis (nls_ses, metadata_10)

**The variable is SES (socioeconomic status).** In logistic regressions of BA degree
completion on raw log-odds coefficients (White = race reference, n = 8,773):

- Model A (race + SES, no academic characteristics): SES = +0.3524 (SE 0.0879, p = 0.0001)
- Model B (race + SES + class percentile + ASVAB ability entered jointly): SES = -0.2316
  (SE 0.1005, classic p = 0.0211; robust HC1 p = 0.0375), i.e., it drops by ~0.58 and
  loses conventional significance once academic characteristics are included together.

SES's positive association with BA completion runs through academic characteristics in
secondary school (class percentile and ASVAB ability, correlated 0.70 with each other and
0.46/0.30 with SES): conditioning on them absorbs and slightly overshoots the SES effect
(suppression), while race coefficients move the opposite way (-0.86/-0.71 -> +0.09/-0.27).
No other variable in the dataset reproduces the +0.36 -> -0.23 pattern.
